confirmed.sharpenedFix:
Make expiry a node removal, not a repaint, and give the one focus case that removal cannot survive a real destination and a word.

In offerUndo (notebook.js:181-188) replace the timeout body:

```js
undoTimer = setTimeout(() => {
  undone = null;
  const strip = mount.querySelector(".undo");
  if (!strip) return;
  const wasInside = strip.contains(document.activeElement);
  strip.remove();
  mount.querySelector(".sheet")?.removeAttribute("data-undo");
  if (wasInside) {
    const back =
      mount.querySelector(".idxRow[data-cursor]") ||
      mount.querySelector(phone.matches ? ".phoneField" : ".topField");
    if (back) back.focus({ preventScroll: true });
    say(N.copy.undoClosed);          // add to notes-copy.ts, e.g. "The way back has closed."
  }
}, UNDO_SECONDS * 1000);
```

Three things this gets right that the original fix did not:
1. `.idxRow[data-cursor]` is a real hook (notebook.js:1191, master.css:564) — the keyboard cursor's own row, so focus lands where the operator last was, not on row one.
2. The field fallback uses the same `phone.matches ? ".phoneField" : ".topField"` pair the rest of the file already uses for refocus (274, 1968, 2005), rather than an undefined selector.
3. Removing `data-undo` releases `--undo-pad` (master.css:630). Do it in the same frame as the node removal so the reserved band and the strip never disagree; the index's scrollTop is untouched by a padding change and needs no restore.

Leave paint() alone for every operator-initiated act.

Then close the hole in the gate. Add to notes-interaction-check.mjs, using Playwright's clock so the assertion costs no wall time:

```js
await page.clock.install();
// capture a note, focus the fifth .idxRow, remember its data-id
await page.clock.fastForward(31_000);
ok("the undo window closing never takes the keyboard with it",
   await page.evaluate(() => document.activeElement?.classList.contains("idxRow")));
// and again with focus parked on .undoAct
ok("focus inside the strip is handed back to the pile, and said out loud", …);
```

Two assertions, and the class of defect — clock-driven repaints, as opposed to act-driven ones — becomes visible to the 333-assertion gate for the first time. Worth sweeping for any other setTimeout that ends in paint() while you are in there.
